# Range 8: the end of the floppy driver's format (function 8), the file functions 12, 13 and
# 15-19 of the table at $0550 (open a control block, close, read and write bytes, read and
# write whole sectors), and the byte and sector transfer machinery under them.
#
# The transfer state at IY+$48-$5A: +$48 the mode (bit 0 read, bit 1 function 17, bit 6 step to
# the next sector first, bit 7 the read met the file's end), +$49/$4A the memory address,
# +$4B/$4C the bytes left, +$4D/$4E the bytes moved, +$4F/$50 the cluster's index in the file,
# +$51 the sector in the cluster, +$52/$53 the offset in the sector, +$54 sectors per cluster,
# +$55/$56 the cluster, +$57/$58 the disk sector, +$59/$5A set by XFER_SETUP.
# A file's control block (IX): +$07 the drive, +$17-$1A the size, +$1B date, +$1D time,
# +$1F/$20 the directory's cluster (bit 6 changed, bit 5 read only), +$21/$22 the first
# cluster, +$23/$24 a cluster kept for the next call, +$25/$26 its index, +$28-$2B the
# position, +$2C the disk's serial.

# --- The format: function 8 of FLOPPY_DRIVER, after FMT_BUILD_IMAGE

@2477 FMT_PASSES
  The format's passes, entered with the track image built at HL': FMT_ALL_TRACKS writes every
  track (IY-$12 = 8), then a second pass with IY-$12 = 0 reads every track back. Then the boot
  sector is built in the same buffer and written to track 0, side 0, sector 1:
  1. BPB_FROM_MEDIA fills in the BPB from the media byte C.
  2. +0 gets the 3 bytes and "EXDOS1.3" at $24C2, +$1E a RET ($C9).
  3. +$40 gets "VOL_ID" ($113D), +$47 the 4 bytes at IY-$4A as the disk's serial.
  4. FD_XFER_RETRY writes it with IY-$12 = 5 (one sector, no verify).
  A double-sided format that finds side 1 missing on track 0 comes back here with the media
  byte one lower (FMT_ONE_SIDED): the one-sided type.
  .2477 Pass 1: every track, the function at IY-$12
  .247A A = 0: pass 2 reads the tracks back
  .247D Pass 2
  .2480 A = the media byte
  .2482 Keeps the buffer
  .2483 C = the media byte
  .2484 The BPB at +$0B; HL = the buffer +$0B
  .2487 DE = the buffer +$0B
  .2488 From the last byte of "EXDOS1.3"
  .248B The jump bytes and the name: 11 bytes
  .248E DE = the buffer +$0A
  .248F Down to the buffer +0
  .2493 HL = the buffer - 1
  .2494 HL = the buffer +$1E
  .2495 A RET
  .2499 HL = the buffer +$40
  .249A DE = it
  .249B "VOL_ID"
  .249E 6 letters
  .24A2 DE = the buffer +$47 (+$46 stays 0)
  .24A3 HL = IY
  .24A6 IY-$4A
  .24AA 4 bytes: the serial
  .24AF HL = the buffer
  .24B1 Keeps the media byte
  .24B2 Track 0, sector 1
  .24B5 Side 0
  .24B6 One sector
  .24B7 Function 5: write
  .24BB Writes the boot sector
  .24BF Failed: map the error
  .24C1 A = 0, Z: formatted

@24D0 FMT_TRACK
  One track: D = the last side (1 for a double-sided media byte), sides done downwards.
  .24D0 A = the media byte
  .24D1 Bit 0: two sides
  .24D3 D = the side

@24D4 FMT_SIDE
  One side of track E: keeps the return address and the track and side, then (pass 1, IY-$12
  bit 3) writes the track and side into the 9 address fields of the image (FMT_PATCH_IDS).
  .24D4 Keeps the pass's return address
  .24D5 Keeps the side and the track
  .24D7 B' = the side, C' = the track
  .24D8 Keeps the image's address
  .24D9 Writing the tracks (function 8)?
  .24DD No: the image is not used
  .24DF 9 address fields
  .24E1 The first one's track byte: the image +$98

@24E4 FMT_PATCH_IDS
  Writes the track and the side into one address field, then steps to the next ($290 bytes on).
  .24E5 The track
  .24E7 The side
  .24E8 One sector's bytes in the image, less 1
  .24EB Next address field

@24EE FMT_SIDE_XFER
  The transfer for one side: L = 9 sectors, or 8 for a media byte with bit 1 set.
  .24EE HL = the image
  .24EF Kept again
  .24F0 The main set
  .24F1 Keeps the media byte
  .24F2 Keeps the side and the track
  .24F3 9 sectors
  .24F5 A media byte for 8 sectors a track?
  .24F9 8

@24FA FMT_SIDE_CALL
  Runs the side's transfer (FD_XFER_RETRY) from sector 1, then takes the registers back: the
  pass's return address goes to HL for JP (HL) at the end.
  .24FA C = the side
  .24FB D = the track
  .24FC From sector 1
  .24FE Writes or reads the side
  .2501 The side and the track
  .2502 The media byte
  .2504 HL' = the image again
  .2506 HL = the pass's return address

@2507 FMT_SIDE_DONE
  After a side: a failure leaves the format through FD_ERR_MAP; Z from $C4B2 leaves it at once;
  else the next side down. After side 0, a double-sided pass 1 on track 0 reads an address
  field on side 1: a time-out or a disk change fails, any other failure, or a field whose side
  byte is not 1, makes the disk one-sided (FMT_ONE_SIDED).
  .2507 Failed: map the error and leave
  .250A $C4B2 says stop?
  .250D Yes: leave the format with Z
  .250E The next side down
  .250F Another side
  .2512 Two sides?
  .2514 No
  .2516 Pass 1 (writing the tracks)?
  .251A No
  .251C Track 0?
  .251E No
  .2520 Keeps the track and the side
  .2521 Keeps the media byte
  .2522 Side 1
  .2524 Reads an address field there (ADDR_READ's $22E5)
  .2527 The media byte
  .2528 The track and the side
  .2529 Read: check its side byte
  .252B Time-out or disk change?
  .252D Yes: fail

@252F FMT_ONE_SIDED
  Formats again with the media byte one lower: bit 0 clear, the one-sided type.
  .252F Media byte - 1: one side
  .2530 Formats the disk again

@2533 FMT_SIDE1_CHK
  An address field was read on side 1: its side byte (IY-$10) must be 1.
  .2533 The field's side byte
  .2536 Side 1?
  .2537 No: the drive has one head

@2539 FMT_NEXT_TRACK
  The track count by the media byte: 40 for bit 2 set, else 80.
  .2539 40 tracks
  .253B A 40-track media byte?
  .253F 80 tracks

@2540 FMT_TRACK_STEP
  The next track; after the last one the pass returns through its return address in HL.
  .2540 The next track
  .2541 Tracks left
  .2542 More: the next track
  .2544 Pass done: back to its caller, A = 0

@254B FILL_PAIR_LOOP
  FILL_PAIRS' inner loop: writes byte A count times, then takes the next pair.
  .254B One byte of the run
  .254D Rest of the run
  .254F B = the pairs left
  .2550 Next pair

# --- Functions 12 and 13: a file's control block and its directory entry

@257D FN13_CLOSE
  EXDOS function 13 (table word at $056A), the call close makes on a file. A file the control
  block marks changed (bit 6 of +$20) gets its directory entry brought up to date:
  1. The directory's cluster (+$1F/$20, flag bits cleared) goes to IY+$30; the file's drive
     (+$07) is selected, its disk checked against the file's.
  2. The entry is searched for again (DIR_FIRST, SEARCH_DIR): missing gives $CF; one with
     attribute bits 0-2 set gives $A1.
  3. STAMP_ENTRY writes the time and date; the archive bit (5) is set; the first cluster (+$21)
     and the size (+$17) are copied in. The drive's changed buffers are written out.
  4. FCB_FROM_ENTRY reloads the block from the entry (position 0).
  Every return goes through $264A: DE = the control block.
  Entry: A = 0 (the dispatcher's); DE = the file's control block; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: A = 0, Z (also for an unchanged file); DE = IX = the control block.
  Error: NZ, A = $CF (the entry is gone), $A1 (Invalid file attributes) or SELECT_DRIVE's
  error.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, all alternates; the directory sector's buffer; IY+$30, IY+$60-$62.
  Keeps: IY.
  .257D IX = the control block
  .2580 Every return: DE = IX
  .2584 DE = the directory's cluster and flags
  .258A Changed?
  .258C No: done
  .258D Clears the changed bit
  .258F And the read-only bit
  .2591 The directory to search
  .2597 C = the file's drive
  .259A B = 1: check it holds the file's disk
  .259C Selects the drive; HL = its record
  .259F Failed?
  .25A1 Starts on the directory
  .25A4 Searches it for the name
  .25A7 Not found: error $CF
  .25A9 Attribute bits 0-2 set?
  .25AB Error $A1 (Invalid file attributes)
  .25AE Time and date into the entry
  .25B1 Keeps the entry
  .25B2 HL = the control block, the record kept
  .25B5 The size at +$17
  .25B9 DE = it, HL = the entry
  .25BA Entry +$0B: the attributes
  .25BD The archive bit
  .25BF Entry +$1A: the first cluster
  .25C2 From the block's +$21
  .25CC HL = the block's +$17, DE = the entry's +$1C
  .25CD 4 bytes: the size
  .25D1 HL = the record
  .25D2 Writes the drive's changed buffers out
  .25D5 DE = the entry
  .25D6 The entry again (its buffer may have moved)
  .25D9 No attributes for the block
  .25DB Reloads the block from the entry

@25DD CLOSE_NOT_FOUND
  The file's name is no longer in its directory: error $CF.
  .25DD Error $CF

@25E0 FN12_OPEN_FCB
  EXDOS function 12 (table word at $0568): fills a file's control block from its directory
  entry. DIR_REOPEN_ENTRY goes back to the entry; a directory or a volume label (attribute bit 4
  or 3) gives $A1; then FCB_FROM_ENTRY copies it in, the position set to 0. Every return goes
  through $264A: DE = the control block.
  Entry: DE = the control block (first byte $FE); HL = the block with the directory position;
  IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: A = 0, Z; DE = IX = the control block; BC = the file's first cluster; IY+$60 = the unit
  (IY+$2B), IY+$61/$62 = the first cluster.
  Error: NZ, A = $A1 (Invalid file attributes) or DIR_REOPEN_ENTRY's error.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, AF', BC', DE', HL'.
  Keeps: IY.
  .25E0 Every return: DE = IX
  .25E4 Back to the entry; A = its attributes
  .25E7 Failed?
  .25E8 B = the attributes
  .25E9 A directory or a volume label?
  .25EB Error $A1 (Invalid file attributes)

@25EE FCB_FROM_ENTRY
  Copies the directory entry DE into the control block IX: the size, date and time, the first
  cluster (also kept as the cluster of index 0), the directory's cluster (IY+$30) with bit 5 set
  for a read-only file (bit 0 of the attributes in B), and position 0. IY+$60-$62 keep the unit
  and the first cluster.
  .25EE Keeps the attributes
  .25EF HL = the control block
  .25F2 +$17: the size
  .25F6 DE = it, HL = the entry
  .25F7 Entry +$1C: its size
  .25FA 4 bytes
  .25FC Keeps the entry's +$1C
  .2600 HL = entry +$1B
  .2601 BC = the first cluster (+$1A)
  .2604 Kept for the end
  .2605 IY+$61/$62 = the first cluster
  .260B The unit being used
  .260E Kept at IY+$60
  .2611 HL = entry +$18: the date
  .2613 Into the block's +$1B
  .2617 HL = entry +$16: the time
  .261B Into the block's +$1D
  .261F HL = the block's +$1F
  .2620 BC = the first cluster
  .2621 DE = IY+$30: the directory's cluster

@2625 FCB_FLAGS
  Clears the flag bits of the directory's cluster and sets bit 5 for a read-only file.
  .2625 Not changed
  .2627 Not read only
  .2629 A = the attributes
  .262A Read only?
  .262E Yes: mark the block

@2630 FCB_STORE
  Writes the rest of the block from +$1F: the directory's cluster and flags, the first cluster
  twice (+$21 and +$23, the cluster kept for index 0), the index 0 (+$25) and the position 0
  (+$28-$2B); +$27 is left as it was. Returns A = 0, Z.
  .2630 +$1F/$20: the directory and the flags
  .2634 +$21/$22: the first cluster
  .2638 +$23/$24: the cluster kept
  .263C A = 0
  .263D +$25/$26: its index, 0
  .2641 Skips +$27
  .2642 +$28-$2B: the position, 0
  .2649 Returns through $264A

# --- Functions 18 and 19: whole sectors, past the buffers

@264E FN18_READ_SECT
  EXDOS function 18 (table word at $0574): reads whole sectors into memory. A = 1, then
  FN19_WRITE_SECT's code.
  Entry: A = 0 (the dispatcher's); DE = the first sector; H = the count; L = the drive; IX = the
  address; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: as FN19_WRITE_SECT.
  Error: as FN19_WRITE_SECT.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, H, IX, all alternates; IY+$48-$4A, IY+$57/$58.
  Keeps: L, IY.
  .264E A = 1: read

@264F FN19_WRITE_SECT
  EXDOS function 19 (table word at $0576), and function 18's body: moves H whole sectors
  between memory at IX and drive L from sector DE, past the sector buffers (RAW_SECTORS), bit 0
  of IY+$48 saying which way (1 reads). A block that would pass $FFFF gives $9B. The drive's
  changed buffers are written out and all its buffers dropped first, and dropped again after.
  Entry: A = 0 (write), 1 from FN18_READ_SECT (read); DE = the first sector; H = the count;
  L = the drive (0 the default, 1-26 A-Z); IX = the address in the caller's pages; IY = EXDOS's
  base.
  Exit: A = 0; H = the count; L = the drive; IX = the address after the last sector; DE = the
  sector after it.
  Error: A = $9B (the block passes $FFFF) or SEL_DRIVE_REC's error; H = 0; IX, DE as on entry.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, H, IX, all alternates; IY+$48-$4A, IY+$57/$58.
  Keeps: L, IY.
  .264F The direction
  .2652 The first sector
  .2658 Keeps the count and the drive
  .2659 BC = the address
  .265C IY+$49/$4A = the address
  .2662 A = the address's high byte
  .2663 Plus 2 x count pages ...
  .2664 Past $FFFF?
  .2666 ... (512 bytes a sector)

@2667 RAWFN_CHECK
  Fails with $9B when the block passes $FFFF; selects drive L and empties its buffers, then
  moves the sectors (RAW_SECTORS).
  .2667 Error $9B (with carry)
  .2669 Past $FFFF: fail
  .266B C = the drive
  .266C Selects it; HL = its record
  .266F Failed?
  .2672 Writes its changed buffers out
  .2675 Drops all its buffers
  .2678 B = the count, C = the drive
  .267A Moves the sectors
  .267D Drops the buffers again
  .2680 HL = the caller's count and drive
  .2681 A = 0: done
  .2682 Skips $2683 (POP HL, LD H,0): the error entry, H = 0
  .2686 DE = IY+$49: the address after the last sector

@268A RAWFN_SET_IX
  IX = the address after the last sector; DE = the next sector.
  .268A IX = it
  .268D DE = IY+$57: the next sector

@2691 RAWFN_EXIT
  Returns with the function's results.

# --- Functions 15-17: bytes of a file

@2692 FN17_WRITE_RD
  EXDOS function 17 (table word at $0572): FN16_WRITE with IY+$48 = 2 (bit 1), so every
  sector written in part is read in first, whatever the file's size.
  Entry: A = 0 (the dispatcher's); as FN16_WRITE.
  Exit: as FN16_WRITE.
  Error: as FN16_WRITE.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, all alternates; the control block's position.
  Keeps: IY.
  .2692 A = 2 (with the next)

@2693 FN15_READ
  EXDOS function 15 (table word at $056E): reads bytes from a file, FN16_WRITE's code with
  IY+$48 = 1.
  Entry: A = 0 (the dispatcher's); as FN16_WRITE.
  Exit: as FN16_WRITE; A = $E4, NZ when the file ended first (BC = the bytes read).
  Error: as FN16_WRITE.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, all alternates; the control block's position.
  Keeps: IY.
  .2693 A = 1: read

@2694 FN16_WRITE
  EXDOS function 16 (table word at $0570), also the body of 15 and 17: moves BC bytes between
  memory at IX and the file DE at its position, IY+$48 the mode (bit 0: 1 reads). Sets up the
  counts and runs XFER_BYTES; then adds the bytes moved to the position (+$28-$2B). A read that
  met the file's end returns $E4 with the bytes it got.
  Entry: A = 0 (the dispatcher's; 1 from $2693, 2 from $2692); BC = the count; DE = the file's
  control block; IX = the address in the caller's pages; IY = EXDOS's base.
  Exit: A = 0, Z; BC = the bytes moved; DE = the control block; IX = HL = the address after
  the last byte.
  Error: NZ, A = $E4 (a read met the file's end) or the error code ($9B the block passes
  $FFFF, $A7 Read only file, $AC Disk full, $9A, the drive's errors).
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, IX, all alternates; the control block's position.
  Keeps: IY.
  .2694 The mode
  .2697 No bytes moved yet
  .269E The bytes left: the count
  .26A4 IX = the control block
  .26A7 HL = the address
  .26A8 Kept at IY+$49/$4A
  .26AE Moves the bytes
  .26B1 BC = the bytes moved
  .26B7 HL = the address after them
  .26BD IX = it, DE = the control block
  .26C1 Keeps the result
  .26C3 The block's +$28: the position
  .26C7 Plus the bytes moved
  .26CE No carry?
  .26D0 Carry into byte 3
  .26D2 No carry?
  .26D4 Carry into byte 4

@26D6 XFER_POS_DONE
  The position is moved on: the result, or $E4 when a read met the file's end.
  .26D6 HL = the address after
  .26D7 The result back
  .26D8 Failed?
  .26DA A read cut at the file's end?
  .26DE No: A = 0
  .26DF $E4: fewer bytes than asked for

@26E2 XFER_BYTES
  Moves the bytes of a read or write ($2694) for the file IX:
  1. Checks the block does not pass $FFFF, selects the file's drive (checking it holds the
     file's disk), and works out where the transfer starts and ends (XFER_SETUP).
  2. Finds the cluster of the start (FILE_CLUSTER_N) and moves the part of the first sector
     from the offset (XFER_PART_SECT).
  3. Moves runs of whole sectors straight between memory and disk (RAW_SECTORS), each run as
     many sectors as lie in consecutive clusters (XFER_LOOP).
  4. Moves the part of the last sector and keeps the cluster reached and its index in the
     block (+$23-$26) for the next call.
  Entry: BC = the count; HL = the address; IX = the control block; IY = EXDOS's base with
  IY+$48-$4E set by $2694.
  Exit: A = 0, Z; IY+$49/$4A = the address after the last byte; IY+$4D/$4E = the bytes moved.
  Error: NZ, A = the error code; carry with A = $9B when the block passes $FFFF.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, HL, all alternates; IY+$4B-$5A; the control block's +$17-$26.
  Keeps: IX, IY.
  .26E2 No bytes?
  .26E4 Done: A = 0
  .26E5 BC = the count - 1
  .26E6 HL = the last byte's address
  .26E7 Error $9B
  .26E9 Past $FFFF: fail
  .26EA C = the file's drive
  .26ED B = 1: check it holds the file's disk
  .26EF Selects the drive; HL = its record
  .26F2 Failed?
  .26F4 Where the transfer starts and ends
  .26F7 DE = IY+$4F: the start's cluster index

@26FB XFER_START
  Finds the start's cluster, then moves the first sector's part when the start is not at a
  sector's beginning.
  .26FB Set up: the start's cluster
  .26FE Kept at IY+$55
  .2704 Failed?
  .2705 BC = the offset in the sector
  .270B Not at a sector's beginning?
  .270D Then that sector's part first
  .2710 Failed?

@2711 XFER_LOOP
  The whole sectors left (the bytes left / 512): with none, the last sector's part
  (XFER_LAST_PART). Otherwise steps to the next sector, and gathers a run: the rest of this
  cluster, then whole clusters while the next one in the chain follows on (XFER_RUN_GROW).
  .2711 C = the bytes left / 256
  .2714 C = the whole sectors left
  .2716 None: the last sector's part
  .2718 Steps on when the last sector was used
  .271B Failed (the chain ended)?
  .271C A = - the sector in the cluster
  .271F B = it
  .2720 DE = IY+$55: the cluster

@2724 XFER_RUN_FIRST
  The disk sector to start from (XFER_SECT_NUM).
  .2724 IY+$57 = the run's first sector

@2727 XFER_RUN_GROW
  B = the run's sectors up to the end of cluster DE. Ends the run when it covers the sectors
  left; else reads the next cluster and grows the run by a cluster while it is DE + 1.
  .2727 Plus the sectors per cluster
  .272B B = the run up to this cluster's end
  .272C Enough for the sectors left?
  .272D Yes
  .272F Keeps the cluster
  .2730 DE = the next cluster
  .2733 HL = the cluster, the record kept
  .2735 HL = the cluster + 1
  .2736 The next one?
  .2738 No: the run ends here
  .273A Yes: the cluster index + 1
  .273D No carry?
  .273F Carry into the high byte

@2742 XFER_RUN_NEXT
  Takes the next cluster into the run.
  .2742 HL = the record
  .2743 Grows the run

@2745 XFER_RUN_BREAK
  The chain jumps: the run ends at the last sector of cluster DE.
  .2745 HL = the cluster + 1
  .2746 DE = it
  .2747 DE = the cluster
  .2748 HL = the record
  .2749 A = the sectors per cluster: its last sector

@274E XFER_RUN_FITS
  The run ends inside this cluster: B = the sectors left.
  .274E A = the sectors per cluster
  .2751 Less the run up to the cluster's end
  .2752 Plus the sectors left: past the run's last sector
  .2753 B = the sectors left

@2754 XFER_RUN_DO
  Records where the run ends (the sector in the cluster, the cluster), moves the run
  (RAW_SECTORS), counts its bytes and goes on.
  .2754 The run's last sector in the cluster
  .2758 The run's last cluster
  .275E Keeps the run's sectors
  .275F Moves the run past the buffers
  .2763 B = the bytes / 256
  .2765 BC = the bytes
  .2767 Counts them
  .276A The last sector is used: step on next
  .276E More sectors?

@2770 XFER_LAST_PART
  The last sector's part from offset 0 (XFER_PART_SECT); then keeps the cluster reached and its
  index in the control block, so the next call starts its walk there.
  .2770 From the sector's beginning
  .2773 Moves the part
  .2776 Failed?
  .2777 DE = IY+$55: the cluster reached

@277B XFER_CACHE_CL
  The control block's +$23/$24 = the cluster reached.
  .2781 DE = IY+$4F: its index

@2785 XFER_CACHE_IDX
  The control block's +$25/$26 = its index; returns A = 0.

@278C XFER_PART_SECT
  Moves the part of one sector from offset BC: to the sector's end, or the bytes left when
  fewer, through a sector buffer (SECT_VIA_BUF). Steps to the next sector first when bit 6 of
  IY+$48 says the current one was used; then sets that bit and counts the bytes.
  Entry: BC = the offset in the sector; HL = the drive's record; IY+$48-$58, the transfer
  state.
  Exit: A = 0, Z (also when no bytes are left).
  Error: NZ, A = $9A (the cluster chain ended).
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, all alternates; IY+$48-$58.
  Keeps: HL, IX, IY.
  .278C Keeps the record
  .278D A sector's bytes
  .2791 Less the offset: the room in the sector
  .2793 DE = the room
  .2794 HL = the bytes left
  .279A More left than room?
  .279C Yes: the room
  .279E No: HL = the bytes left
  .279F DE = them

@27A1 PART_SECT_DO
  Moves DE bytes of the sector, unless there are none.
  .27A1 No bytes?
  .27A3 HL = the record
  .27A4 None: done
  .27A5 Steps on when the last sector was used
  .27A8 Failed (the chain ended)?
  .27A9 IY+$57 = the sector
  .27AC Moves the bytes through a buffer
  .27AF The sector is used: step on next
  .27B3 Counts the bytes
  .27B6 A = 0, Z

@27B8 RAW_SECTORS
  Moves B whole sectors between the disk and memory past the sector buffers, from sector
  IY+$57 and the address at IY+$49 (bit 0 of IY+$48: 1 reads, 0 writes).
  1. The address is mapped into page 1 (MAP_TO_PAGE1). The run is the sectors that fit before
     page 1's end, at most B.
  2. The unit's driver runs request 4 (read) or WRITE_REQUEST's 5/6 on the run (UNIT_REQUEST:
     B the sectors, C the segment, DE the sector, IX the address in page 1). A failure asks
     DISK_ERROR: a retry runs the rest of the run again, an Ignore skips it (segment 0: no
     request is made).
  3. RAW_SYNC_BUFS squares the buffers with the run; IY+$49 and IY+$57 move past it.
  4. A sector that would cross page 1's end goes alone through SECT_VIA_BUF.
  Entry: B = the sectors; HL = the drive's record; IY+$48, IY+$49/$4A, IY+$57/$58; IY.
  Exit: A = 0, Z; IY+$49/$4A and IY+$57/$58 past the sectors.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, all alternates.
  Keeps: HL, IX, IY.
  .27B8 No sectors (left)?
  .27BA Done
  .27BB DE = IY+$49: the address

@27BF RAW_MAP
  Maps the address into page 1 and sizes the run: the whole sectors before $8000, at most B.
  .27BF A = the segment, DE = the address in page 1
  .27C2 A' = the segment
  .27C3 Keeps the record
  .27C4 Room before $8000, less one sector
  .27C8 A sector crosses page 1's end?
  .27CA Yes: that one through a buffer
  .27CC A = the room / 256
  .27CD The room in sectors ...
  .27CF ... plus the last one
  .27D0 Fewer than B?
  .27D3 No: all B

@27D4 RAW_RUN_SIZE
  C = the run; the sectors left after it are kept with it.
  .27D4 C = the run
  .27D5 HL = the record
  .27D6 Run - B ...
  .27D7 ... negated: the sectors left after it
  .27D9 B = them
  .27DA Kept with the run
  .27DB B = the run
  .27DC A = the segment
  .27DD C = the segment

@27DF RAW_RUN_TRY
  One try at the run: B = the sectors, C = the segment, DE = the address in page 1.
  .27DF The run and the segment, for the end
  .27E0 And again, for this try
  .27E1 The address, for the end
  .27E2 IX = the address, the caller's IX kept
  .27E4 Request 4: read
  .27E6 A write?
  .27EA Request 5 or 6
  .27ED DE = IY+$57: the sector

@27F1 RAW_RUN_REQ
  Runs the request (not for segment 0: a run the user chose to ignore), then works out where
  it got to: IY+$57 past the sectors done, DE past their bytes, B = the sectors not done.
  .27F1 Keeps the sector
  .27F2 Segment 0?
  .27F4 No: the driver moves the sectors
  .27F7 Keeps the result
  .27F8 DE = the sector
  .27F9 The caller's IX
  .27FA Keeps the record
  .27FC HL = the sectors done
  .27FF Plus the first sector
  .2800 IY+$57 = the next sector
  .2806 HL = the record
  .2807 DE = the address
  .2808 D + 2 x the sectors done ...
  .280B ... the address after them
  .280C A = the sectors done
  .280D B = the run, C = the segment
  .280E Done - run ...
  .280F ... negated: the sectors not done
  .2811 B = them
  .2812 The result
  .2813 Done?
  .2815 NC: not a format error
  .2816 Asks: Z retries, NZ goes on
  .2819 Retry?
  .281B Ignore: the rest is skipped (segment 0)

@281D RAW_RUN_RETRY
  Tries the rest of the run again.
  .281D Again

@281F RAW_RUN_DONE
  The run is done: squares the buffers with it, moves IY+$49 past it and goes on with the
  sectors left.
  .281F B = the run, C = the segment
  .2820 Squares the buffers with the run
  .2823 B = the sectors left, C = the run
  .2824 The address's high byte ...
  .2827 ... plus 2 x the run (512 bytes a sector)

@282C RAW_LOOP
  The next run.
  .282C More sectors?

@282E RAW_SPLIT_SECT
  A sector that would cross page 1's end: moved alone through a buffer, then the next sector.
  .282E HL = the record
  .282F One sector fewer
  .2830 Kept
  .2831 A whole sector
  .2834 From offset 0
  .2836 Moves it through a buffer
  .2839 The sectors left
  .283A The next sector
  .283D No carry?
  .283F Carry into the high byte

@2842 RAW_SPLIT_NEXT
  Goes on with the sectors left.
  .2842 More sectors?

@2844 RAW_SYNC_BUFS
  After a run of sectors moved past the buffers, squares the drive's buffers with it: a buffer
  whose sector is one of the run's (the B sectors before IY+$57) is dropped after a write
  (tag 0, to the head of the chain); after a read, a changed one has its data copied over that
  sector's place in memory (segment C in page 1), so memory gets the newer data.
  Entry: B = the run's sectors; C = the segment; DE = the address after the run in page 1;
  HL = the drive's record; IY+$57 = the sector after the run; IY+$48 bit 0 the direction.
  Exit: none.
  Changes: AF, BC', DE', HL'.
  Keeps: BC, DE, HL, IX, IY.
  .2844 Marks the drive's buffers

@2847 SYNC_BUF_LOOP
  Takes the next marked buffer and checks whether its sector is in the run.
  .2847 Keeps the run and the segment
  .2849 The next marked buffer
  .284C None left: done
  .284E HL = the buffer +4: its sector
  .2852 DE = the sector
  .2855 HL = the sector, DE = the buffer +5
  .2856 BC = the sector after the run
  .285C Sector - it (negative in the run)
  .285E B' = the run, C' = the segment
  .285F Less than 256 below it?
  .2861 No: not in the run
  .2863 Within the run's sectors?
  .2865 No
  .2867 DE = the buffer +3
  .2869 HL = the buffer +3, DE = the distance
  .286A A read?
  .2870 Buffer +2 ...
  .2871 ... the tag 0: free
  .2875 To the head of the chain

@2878 SYNC_BUF_NEXT
  The next buffer.
  .2878 The main set again

@287B SYNC_BUF_COPY
  A read: a changed buffer (bit 7 of its +3) has its 512 bytes copied over the sector's place
  in memory, with the run's segment in page 1.
  .287B Changed?
  .287D No: memory is right
  .287F B = the segment
  .2882 A = page 1's segment
  .2884 The run's segment in page 1
  .2887 The address after the run
  .2889 The buffer's data: +$0B
  .288D HL = the address after, the data kept
  .288E D = the distance (negative sectors)
  .288F E = 0
  .2890 Less 512 bytes ...
  .2891 ... for each sector: the sector's place
  .2892 DE = it
  .2893 HL = the buffer's data
  .2894 A sector
  .2899 Page 1 back

@289D SYNC_BUF_END
  No more marked buffers.
  .289D The main set
  .289E The caller's BC

@28A0 XFER_STEP_SECT
  Moves the transfer on by one sector when bit 6 of IY+$48 says the current one was used: the
  next sector of the cluster (IY+$51), or at the cluster's end sector 0 of the next cluster in
  the chain (IY+$55, its index IY+$4F + 1).
  Entry: HL = the drive's record; IY.
  Exit: A = 0, Z; bit 6 of IY+$48 clear.
  Error: NZ, A = $9A when the chain ends.
  Changes: AF, all alternates.
  Keeps: BC, DE, HL, IX, IY.
  .28A0 A = 0
  .28A1 The current sector used?
  .28A5 No: stay
  .28A6 Not any more
  .28AA The next sector of the cluster
  .28AE Past the cluster's end?
  .28B1 No
  .28B3 Keeps DE
  .28B4 DE = IY+$4F: the cluster's index

@28B8 STEP_NEXT_CL
  The next cluster: its index + 1, then the cluster itself from the FAT.
  .28B8 The index + 1
  .28BF DE = IY+$55: the cluster

@28C3 STEP_READ_FAT
  Reads the next cluster: an end mark fails with $9A.
  .28C3 DE = the next cluster
  .28C6 The current cluster
  .28CC The chain ended?
  .28CF Error $9A
  .28D2 Sector 0 of the cluster

@28D3 STEP_SET_SECT
  Stores the sector in the cluster; A = 0, Z.
  .28D3 The sector in the cluster
  .28D6 A = 0, Z

@28D8 XFER_SECT_NUM
  IY+$57/$58 = the disk sector of sector IY+$51 of cluster DE (CLUSTER_TO_SECT).
  Entry: DE = the cluster; HL = the drive's record; IY.
  Exit: IY+$57/$58 = the sector.
  Changes: AF.
  Keeps: BC, DE, HL, IX, IY.
  .28D8 Keeps the cluster
  .28D9 A = the sector in the cluster
  .28DC DE = IY+$55 (the cluster again)

@28E0 SECT_NUM_STORE
  Works out the disk sector and keeps it.
  .28E0 DE = the disk sector
  .28E9 The cluster back

@28EB SECT_VIA_BUF
  Moves DE bytes (up to 512) between memory at IY+$49 and sector IY+$57 from offset BC,
  through a sector buffer (GET_SECTOR_BUF). The sector is read in first for a read, for
  function 17 (bit 1 of IY+$48), and for a write that does not cover the sector's data (not
  the whole sector, by the offset and the bytes left measured against IY+$59/$5A). The memory
  is paged into page 1 a 16K page at a time (MAP_TO_PAGE1). Then IY+$49 moves past the bytes; a
  write marks the buffer changed; a buffer used to its end is written back and goes to the head
  of the chain, to be reused first.
  Entry: BC = the offset in the sector; DE = the count; HL = the drive's record; IY+$48,
  IY+$49/$4A, IY+$4B/$4C, IY+$57/$58, IY+$59/$5A; IY.
  Exit: BC = the count; IY+$49/$4A = the address after the bytes; page 1 restored.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, all alternates; the sector's buffer.
  Keeps: HL, IX, IY.
  .28EB IX = the record, the caller's IX kept
  .28EE Keeps the count
  .28EF Keeps the offset
  .28F0 A read?
  .28F4 Yes: read the sector in
  .28F6 A whole sector?
  .28F8 Yes: no need to read it
  .28FA Function 17?
  .28FE Yes: read it in
  .2900 Keeps the count
  .2901 HL = the bytes left
  .2907 DE = IY+$59

@290B SVB_SIZE_CHK
  Decides from the offset and the bytes left against IY+$59 whether the sector must be read.
  .290B Bytes left - IY+$59
  .290E DE = the count
  .290F Below it?
  .2911 Offset 0?
  .2913 No: read it in
  .2915 Less the count too
  .2917 Below: no need to read it
  .2919 Read it in

@291B SVB_SHORT
  The bytes left are below IY+$59: read in unless adding the offset carries.
  .291B Plus the offset
  .291C No carry: no need to read it

@291E SVB_READ_IN
  B = 3: the sector is read in (Ignore allowed); $2921, inside LD HL,$0206, is LD B,2: the
  buffer is taken without reading.
  .291E Read it in, Ignore allowed
  .2920 $2921: LD B,2, no read
  .2923 DE = IY+$57: the sector

@2927 SVB_GET_BUF
  Gets the sector's buffer and points HL at the offset in its data; DE = the address.
  .2927 HL = the sector's buffer
  .292A DE = the offset
  .292B Plus the buffer
  .292C Plus its header: the data's byte
  .2930 BC = the count
  .2931 Kept for the end
  .2933 Page 1's segment
  .2935 Kept for the end
  .2936 DE = IY+$49: the address

@293A SVB_COPY
  Keeps the address for the end.
  .293A The address

@293B SVB_COPY_LOOP
  Copies what fits in the page of address DE: maps it into page 1, the count cut at $8000.
  .293B The address in the caller's pages
  .293C The count
  .293D The buffer's place
  .293E A = the segment, DE = the address in page 1
  .2941 Paged into page 1
  .2943 $8000 - the address - the count
  .294B Fits before $8000?
  .294D No: BC = the room
  .294E Up to $8000

@2950 SVB_DIRECTION
  HL = the buffer, DE = memory for a read; swapped for a write.
  .2950 HL = the buffer's place
  .2951 Keeps the part's size
  .2952 A read?
  .2958 Write: memory to the buffer

@2959 SVB_LDIR
  Copies the part.
  .2959 Copies the part
  .295B A read? (BIT's Z still)
  .295D Write: HL = the buffer, DE = memory

@295E SVB_COPY_NEXT
  The count left after the part: none ends the copy, else the next page of the address.
  .295E BC = the part's size
  .295F HL = the count, the buffer's place kept
  .2961 Less the part
  .2963 BC = the count left
  .2965 HL = the buffer's place
  .2966 All copied?
  .2968 A = the part's address (high byte)
  .2969 Its page
  .296B The next page
  .296D DE = its beginning
  .296E Copies the next part

@2970 SVB_DONE
  Puts page 1 back, moves IY+$49 past the bytes, marks the buffer changed for a write, and
  writes a buffer used to its end back, to the head of the chain.
  .2970 Drops the loop's address
  .2971 HL = the first address
  .2972 Page 1's segment
  .2973 Page 1 back
  .2975 BC = the count
  .2976 HL = the address after the bytes
  .2977 Kept at IY+$49/$4A
  .297D A write?
  .2981 Marks the buffer changed
  .2984 HL = the offset
  .2985 Plus the count
  .2986 Reached the sector's end?
  .2988 No
  .298A HL = the buffer used last
  .2990 Written back, to the head of the chain

@2993 SVB_EXIT
  The caller's IX and HL back.
  .2993 IX back, HL = the record

@2997 XFER_COUNT
  Counts BC bytes as moved: IY+$4D/$4E plus BC; IY+$4B/$4C (the bytes left) minus BC, with the
  carry of the first sum (0 while the total stays below 64K).
  Entry: BC = the bytes; IY.
  Exit: IY+$4B-$4E updated.
  Changes: F.
  Keeps: A, BC, DE, HL, IX, IY.
  .2998 HL = the bytes moved
  .299E Plus these
  .29A5 HL = the bytes left
  .29AB Less these

@29B5 MAP_TO_PAGE1
  Maps an address of the caller's into page 1: A = the segment that holds address DE, from a
  table of four page segments chosen by IY-$80 (1: the four bytes at $BFFC; 2: IY-$5D; 3:
  IY-$36; else IY+$2C) and indexed by the address's page (DE's top two bits); D is moved into
  $40-$7F, the same place in page 1.
  Entry: DE = the address; IY.
  Exit: A = the segment; DE = the address in page 1.
  Changes: AF, D.
  Keeps: BC, HL, IX, IY.
  .29B5 A = the table's kind

@29CF MAP_FROM_IY
  HL = IY + the table's offset.

@29D3 MAP_INDEX
  A = the segment of the address's page; the address moved into page 1.
  .29D3 A = the address's high byte
  .29D4 Top two bits ...
  .29D6 ... the page, 0-3
  .29DB HL = its entry
  .29DC A = the segment
  .29DD The address ...
  .29DF ... in page 1

@29E4 XFER_SETUP
  Prepares a transfer of IY+$4B bytes at the file's position (+$28-$2B):
  1. POS_TO_CLUSTER splits the position: the cluster's index, the sector in it and the offset
     go to IY+$4F-$53.
  2. The same for the transfer's last byte, then for the file's last byte (the size - 1, at
     +$17-$1A) ($2A1A on).
  3. A read of an empty file gives $E4; a read past the end is cut to the end, with bit 7 of
     IY+$48 set to report $E4 after.
  4. A write gives $A7 for a read-only file; else it marks the file changed (bit 6 of +$20) and
     grows the cluster chain to cover the last byte ($AC when the disk is full), with the new
     size.
  Entry: HL = the drive's record; IX = the control block; IY+$48, IY+$4B/$4C; IY.
  Exit: A = 0, Z; IY+$4F-$54 and IY+$59/$5A set.
  Error: NZ, A = the error code ($9A, $A7, $AC or $E4).
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, all alternates; the control block's +$17-$26 for a write.
  Keeps: HL, IX, IY.
  .29E4 Keeps the record
  .29E5 BC = the position's low word
  .29EB DE = its high word
  .29F1 Kept
  .29F3 Splits the start
  .29F6 Too far: fail
  .29F8 The start's cluster index
  .29FE The sector in the cluster
  .2A01 The offset in the sector
  .2A07 HL = the position's low word
  .2A08 DE = IY+$4B: the bytes

@2A0C XFER_END_POS
  The position of the transfer's last byte: the position + the count - 1.
  .2A0C The count - 1
  .2A0D Plus the low word
  .2A0E HL = the high word, the sum kept
  .2A0F BC = the last byte's low word
  .2A10 DE = the high word, HL = the count - 1
  .2A11 HL = the record, the count - 1 kept
  .2A12 Carry out of the low word?
  .2A14 Into the high word

@2A15 XFER_END_CL
  Splits the last byte's position (POS_TO_CLUSTER).
  .2A15 Keeps the last byte's position
  .2A17 Splits it

@2A1A XFER_SIZE_CHK
  A failure leaves; else the file's size - 1 (DE:BC, from +$17-$1A) for the comparison at
  $2A31; an empty file gives $FFFF:$FFFF with Z.
  .2A1A Too far: fail
  .2A1C Keeps the last byte's cluster index
  .2A1D BC = the size's low word
  .2A23 DE = its high word
  .2A29 Low word 0?
  .2A2B Low word - 1
  .2A2C No: no borrow
  .2A2E A = the high word (Z for an empty file)
  .2A30 Borrow from the high word

@2AF8 POS_TO_CLUSTER
  Splits a byte position of a file: the offset in its sector (position mod 512), the sector in
  the cluster, and the cluster's index in the file; IY+$54 = the sectors per cluster.
  Entry: DE:BC = the position (DE the high word); HL = the drive's record (+$0A sectors per
  cluster - 1, +$0B their power of 2); IY.
  Exit: Z; DE = the cluster's index; A = the sector in the cluster; BC = the offset in the
  sector.
  Error: NZ, A = $9A when the index is above $7FFF.
  Changes: AF, B, DE.
  Keeps: C, HL, IX, IY.
  .2AF8 Keeps the record
  .2AF9 A = bits 8-15
  .2AFA D:E:A = the position / 2 ...
  .2AFE ... / 256: the sector in the file
  .2AFF B = bit 8: BC = the offset in the sector
  .2B03 Kept
  .2B04 The record's +$0A
  .2B08 Keeps the sector's low byte
  .2B09 The sector in the cluster
  .2B0A C = it
  .2B0B The sectors per cluster - 1 ...
  .2B0C ... + 1
  .2B0D Kept at IY+$54
  .2B10 A = the sector's low byte
  .2B11 The record's +$0B
  .2B12 B = the power of 2 ...
  .2B13 ... + 1 for DJNZ
  .2B14 Divides by the sectors per cluster

@2B2E FILE_CLUSTER_N
  Finds the cluster of index DE in the file's chain (0: the first cluster), walking the FAT from
  the cluster kept at +$23 (index +$25) when that is not past it, else from the first cluster
  (+$21).
  Entry: DE = the index; HL = the drive's record; IX = the control block; IY.
  Exit: A = 0, Z; DE = the cluster.
  Error: NZ, A = $9A when the block keeps no cluster (+$23 = 0, an empty file) or the chain
  ends first.
  Changes: AF, BC, DE, all alternates.
  Keeps: HL, IX, IY.
  .2B2E Keeps the record
  .2B2F HL = the index
  .2B30 DE = the cluster kept
  .2B36 None?
  .2B38 Error $9A
  .2B3A BC = its index
  .2B40 Steps from it
  .2B42 Not past it: walk from there
  .2B44 HL = the index again
  .2B45 Walk from the first cluster

# --- Notes for the session

question 2692 Function 17 is a write that reads in every sector it writes in part: what calls it, and why not function 16?
question 29B5 Which callers set IY-$80 to 2 and 3, and whose page segments do IY-$5D and IY-$36 hold?
feedback wrong 1FD3 Range 4's FLOPPY_DRIVER says function 7 goes to $241D: the code (SUB 2, DEC, SUB 4, DEC) sends 8 there and makes 7 fail with $BD, which range 8's IY-$12 = 8 test needs
feedback missing 24CD The cards of $2477-$2544 do not say that $24CD pops its caller's address and $2544 returns with JP (HL): every card lists "no return is reachable" or a foreign stack
feedback pattern 26E2-2B5D The transfer state at IY+$48-$5A and the control block's fields (+$17-$2C) recur on 40 cards with no shared summary
feedback listing 2440-2B50 Opened to follow the format's passes, the byte transfer across ranges 8-9 and the run of hidden entries ($2683, $2921)
